<header><p class="st-kicker">sultanate · Maritime Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Bulungan Sultanate</h1><p class="st-meta">1731 CE – 1964 CE (approx.) · capital Tanjung Palas</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Sultanate of north-eastern Borneo on the Kayan river, whose palace was destroyed by the Indonesian army in 1964.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Bulungan grew from a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kayan-people-borneo">Kayan Dayak</a> chiefdom on the Kayan river of north-east <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borneo">Borneo</a>. Around 1731 its ruler Wira Amir converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> and took the title <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> Amiril Mukminin, founding the sultanate at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tanjung-palas">Tanjung Palas</a>. Its early rulers were often under the influence of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/sulu-sultanate">Sulu Sultanate</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berau-sultanate">Berau</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The sultans governed a mixed Kayan, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tidung-people">Tidung</a> and Malay population, exporting forest products, birds nests and rattan. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> contracts in the 19th century made Bulungan a self-governing domain of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/dutch-east-indies">Dutch East Indies</a>, and the discovery of oil on Tarakan island early in the 20th century gave the region new importance.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Bulungan joined <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/indonesia">Indonesia</a> after independence. In 1964, during the Confrontation with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/malaysia">Malaysia</a>, the army accused the court of links with Malaysia, burned the palace and killed members of the royal family, ending the sultanate. The area now forms part of North Kalimantan province.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/bulungan-sultanate.jpg" alt="Bulungan Sultanate" width="320" height="212"><figcaption>Image: Original flag creator by:UnknownVectorized by:Gabpzxs · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Bulungan_Sultanate.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1731 CE – 1964 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>sultanate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Tanjung Palas</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Wira Amir (Amiril Mukminin)</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Bulungan Malay, Kayan</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/indonesia">Indonesia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Sultanate of Bulungan</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1848&amp;focus=bulungan-sultanate" aria-label="Bulungan Sultanate on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7933 2065 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Bulungan Sultanate lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M8253 2321L7959 2663M8253 2321L7959 2664"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7959 2663h0M7959 2664h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8253 2321h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8253 2321h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its capital. <a href="/atlas?year=1848&amp;focus=bulungan-sultanate">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
